WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:07.800
NICK DE CRISTOFARO:
Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.350
My name is Nick De Cristofaro.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:12.050
I'm a Customer Engineer Network
Specialist with Google Cloud,

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:14.580
and I'll be your presenter
today in this video.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.640
If you started your journey
towards Google Cloud

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.220
from a hybrid
on-premesis model, it's

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:21.620
likely that you're already
using some hybrid connectivity

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.530
features, one of
them being Cloud VPN.

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:29.180
Historically, Cloud VPN
offered you an SLA of 99.9%,

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.330
and with the introduction
of our HA VPN solution,

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:36.200
we now offer an SLA of 99.99%.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.460
You're able to benefit
from higher availability,

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.100
as well as ease of migration
between classic VPN and HA VPN

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.020
In this video, I will
show you how easy

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.360
it is to migrate an
existing classic VPN

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.820
tunnel to an HA solution.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.890
First, let's look
at two migrations

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:53.850
path we will look at today.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:57.320
The first one is migrate an
existing classic VPN solution

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:00.930
to HA VPN using BGP as
the routing protocol

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:04.790
to on-premises network or
any non Google networks.

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:08.720
And the second is migrate an
existing classic VPN utilizing

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.380
policy-based VPN
to HA VPN utilizing

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:17.510
BGP between two projects or VPC
within Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.540
Now, let's begin by looking
at the first example migration

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:22.950
path.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.820
As you can see here, I'm in
the hybrid connectivity section

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:27.440
of the Google Cloud console.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.200
Here's a tab for
VPN, and this is

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.390
where I would like to have
a look at the VPN tunnels

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:35.190
and the Cloud VPN gateways
that are available.

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:38.150
Here, you can see that I have
two classic VPN tunnels that

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:39.770
are already established.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:41.690
If I scroll to
the right, you can

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:44.720
see that I have
dynamic BGP VPN tunnel,

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.840
and I have a policy
based VPN tunnel.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.100
For the first section
of this video,

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.310
we will focus on the
demo VPN tunnel that's

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:54.650
already established over BGP.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:57.530
The first thing you want to do
is to create a new Cloud VPN

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.000
gateway.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.210
If I click the Cloud VPN
tab, gateway here, you

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:05.360
can see that I have a classic
VPN gateway with an associated

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:10.080
IP address, and I have an HA
VPN gateway already created.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.870
If I click create
new VPN gateway,

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.960
this is where you would
create your new VPN

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.790
gateway for HA VPN.

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:20.030
You would give it a name
under the VPN gateway name

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.120
and select an appropriate VPC
network for the VPN gateway,

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.750
and these would be the same
VPC network and region that

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.910
match your current classic VPN.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:33.380
My current classic VPN gateway
is configured in us-east4,

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.780
so I would select the us-east4.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:38.360
I will not click Create, because
I've already created one,

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.520
but in your case, you
would click Create.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.250
So next, if I go back to
the Cloud VPN Tunnels tab,

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.480
I'm asked to select
a VPN gateway,

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:50.170
and I will select the
HA VPN high availability

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:53.170
gateway I just created
and click Continue.

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:55.440
I'm presented next
with two options.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.630
In the first example, we will
look at an on-premises or non

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:00.790
Google Cloud peer gateway.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:04.450
I've already created a new
demo non VPN gateway, however

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:08.020
in your case, you would need to
click Create peer VPN gateway

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.560
and then provide a name.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:12.430
In this case, it would be
the on-prem VPN gateway,

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:15.760
and then specify the IP
address of the interfaces

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.010
of your on-premise VPN gateway.

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:22.090
Now, you may have one,
two, or four interfaces,

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.360
and this depends on your
vendor's configuration.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:25.840
In my test
environment, I'm going

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:29.110
to use two interfaces
with the same IP address.

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.570
I'm not going to enter these
right now and click Create,

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:33.250
because I've already done so.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:35.510
I will click Cancel instead.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:39.010
Instead, I will click
peer VPN gateway name

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.740
and shoot the VPN gateway
I've already created.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:43.450
The next step is
to ask me what I

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:46.180
would like to do with high
availability component.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:51.820
Now, high availability, or HA
VPN, provides a 99.99% SLA,

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.730
and therefore it's required to
create a pair of VPN tunnels

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:56.980
to achieve that SLA.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.770
Next, the only routing
option we have for HA VPN

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.050
is dynamic via BGP.

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:04.860
Next, we want to
select a Cloud Router.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.520
You can reuse the
same Cloud Router

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.790
that you were using previously
for the classic VPN BGP

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:10.368
tunnels.

00:04:10.368 --> 00:04:12.160
If you'd like to create
a new Cloud Router,

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:13.930
however, it's not necessary.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:16.390
Here, I will select the
existing Cloud Router,

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:17.964
and next, I will
create the tunnels.

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:24.335
As I select Edit
on the VPN tunnels,

00:04:24.335 --> 00:04:25.710
you can see that
it automatically

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:29.190
populates the associated
interfaces from my HA VPN

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:31.140
gateway that was
created earlier,

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:34.002
and it associates the peer
gateway interfaces for me.

00:04:34.002 --> 00:04:35.460
If you remember,
I mentioned that I

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.770
have two interfaces
for my peer gateway,

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:39.950
and these interfaces
have the same IP address

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:42.150
for the purposes of this demo.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:43.800
For the first tunnel,
I will associate

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:46.650
interface zero of
the HA VPN gateway

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:49.260
with interface zero
of the peer gateway.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:50.800
I'll give it a name.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:53.145
Let's call it havpn-tunnel-0.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:58.290
Next, you can select
the IKE version.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:01.140
Most modern VPN
gateways support IKE V2,

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:02.580
so I'll leave it at that.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:05.140
Next, enter a pre-shared key.

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:06.900
Now, for my example,
the pre-shared key

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:10.170
is going to be demovpnhavpn.

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:11.840
However, please note
the best practice

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.220
is to create a strong
IKE pre-shared key.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:17.590
See links to our documentations
below for more information.

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:21.000
The Generate and copy button
here can create one for you.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:22.740
Now click done.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:25.170
Next, I'll configure
the second tunnel.

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:28.440
As you can see, this is
associated with interface one

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.350
on both the HA VPN
gateway and peer gateway.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:33.000
Following the same
naming convention,

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.280
we will call this
havpn-tunnel-1.

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:43.365
I'll enter the same
pre-shared key and click done.

00:05:43.365 --> 00:05:44.990
Now, once you've
configured everything,

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:48.730
you can click
Create and Continue.

00:05:48.730 --> 00:05:51.910
In the next screen, we have
to configure the BGP sessions.

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:55.570
Let's configure the BGP session
for the first Cloud VPN tunnel.

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:57.230
Click Configure.

00:05:57.230 --> 00:05:58.580
Here, I will give it a name.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:02.570
Let's call it havpn-bgp-0.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:04.845
Now, fill in your peer
autonomous system number.

00:06:04.845 --> 00:06:06.220
That's what's
actually configured

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:07.840
on your on-premises environment.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:11.770
In my environment, it's 65506.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:14.980
Next, let's look at the multi
exit discriminator value

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:16.605
or advertiser out priority.

00:06:16.605 --> 00:06:17.980
Because we would
like to minimize

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:20.050
the downtime and the
recommended configuration

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:22.468
as an active-active
tunnel, I know

00:06:22.468 --> 00:06:24.010
that I've configured
my default route

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:27.520
priority for my classic
VPN tunnel to 100.

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:29.590
So I'll configure the
route priority here

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:32.470
to 1,000 for the HA VPN tunnel.

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:35.050
What this means is that
when these tunnels come up,

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:37.240
traffic will not
automatically switch over

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:39.400
to these interfaces,
because the other tunnel has

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:40.660
better priority.

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:42.310
This effectively
means that we do not

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:43.960
need to move traffic
to the new tunnels

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:45.730
until we're ready to do so.

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:47.223
Now, for all
configuration changes

00:06:47.223 --> 00:06:48.640
that you would do
in the Cloud, we

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:51.670
would recommend them to be done
during maintenance windows.

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:56.710
Next, configure the Cloud
Router BGP IP and peer BGP IP.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:58.650
The IPs depend on
what is configured

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:00.280
in your on-premises environment.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:07.250
In my case, the local Cloud
Router BGP IP is 169.254.4.1,

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:13.876
and the BGP peer
IP is 169.254.4.2.

00:07:13.876 --> 00:07:16.340
Next, you can configure
the advertised routes,

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:18.980
so you may decide to
advertise only certain routes

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:20.330
across this tunnel.

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:22.105
For the purpose of
this demonstration,

00:07:22.105 --> 00:07:23.730
we're not going to
change the defaults,

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:26.180
and then we'll click
Save and Continue.

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:28.310
Next, configure the
second BGP session

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:29.990
with the desired parameters.

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:33.440
Here, I'll configure it with
a similar naming convention.

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:37.750
So here, I will
name it havpn-bgp-1.

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:42.110
The peer ASN is going
to be the same, 65506.

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:44.390
Next, under the
route priority, we'll

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:47.000
be putting the same MED
values so that the tunnels are

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:49.400
in an active-active state,
so they're essentially

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:51.340
able to load balanced traffic.

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:53.740
I will enter value
1000, the same I entered

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:56.705
in the other tunnel, and then
again for Cloud Router BGP

00:07:56.705 --> 00:08:00.140
IP and BGP peer IP, this
depends on your configuration.

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:09.100
For me, it's 169.254.5.1, and
the peer IP is 169.254.5.2.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.960
Now again, let's click
Save and Continue.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:15.394
Finally, let's click the button
to save the BGP configuration.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:20.820
Now here on step
three, we're actually

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:23.280
given a summary page of the
current status of our VPN

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:23.970
tunnels.

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:26.010
You can see that right
now they're establishing,

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:29.100
so it may take roughly 20
to 30 seconds to bring up

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:31.290
the tunnels, so we'll
click OK and return

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:33.480
to the previous page.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:36.250
Now again on this page, you can
see a summary of my Cloud VPN

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:36.750
tunnels.

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:39.630
And as you can see,
my right two tunnels

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:43.140
are waiting for the BGP
peer to be established.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:45.718
You may click Refresh here
at the top of the page,

00:08:45.718 --> 00:08:47.260
and I'll actually
refresh the screen,

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:49.600
so we'll know whether the
BGP has been established.

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:52.390
So let's click
Refresh, and now you

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:56.350
can see that the
BGPs are established.

00:08:56.350 --> 00:08:58.140
So next, if we select
the hamburger menu

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:06.880
at the top left hand side, and
we scroll down to VPC network,

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:09.490
and then under the Networking
section, we will select Routes.

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:14.540
Now in the Routes tab, I'm
interested in the Dynamic tab

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:18.126
to look at the new routes that
were learned across HA VPN.

00:09:18.126 --> 00:09:20.450
If I click Dynamic
tab here, I can

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:23.540
see that I'm learning a new
IP prefix from both tunnels

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:25.430
with priority 1000.

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:28.980
Based on the other BGP routing
priority, which is set to 100,

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:30.830
this means that the
existing classic VPN

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:32.930
tunnel that I had
previously created

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:34.430
would still take precedence.

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:36.650
Recall that a lower
value is better here,

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:39.020
meaning that 100
wins over 1,000.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:42.140
Traffic is still going to flow
across the existing VPN tunnel

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:45.110
and not across the
new HA VPN tunnel.

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:47.720
So next, as you can see here,
I have a demo virtual machine

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:50.180
that I'm going to
establish a ping between it

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:53.460
and my on-premises environment
across that VPN tunnel.

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:59.182
So here, I'm going to initiate
a ping to 192.168.125.1.

00:09:59.182 --> 00:10:00.890
And as you can see,
I'm receiving packets

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:02.750
from that on-premises
machine, which

00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:08.840
is located across the VPN tunnel
on my on-premises network.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:11.020
So next, to have seamless
traffic failover,

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:12.520
let's change your
routing priorities

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:14.530
so that the classic
VPN tunnel has

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:19.630
lower priority or a higher MED
value than the HA VPNs tunnels.

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:25.200
So if you click on the hamburger
menu at the top left hand side,

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:28.900
go back to Networking, click
Hybrid Connectivity, and then

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:35.390
VPN, select the existing
classic VPN tunnel,

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:37.760
scroll down to the
Routing and Security

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:42.410
section, and the line item
BGP session, click Modify BGP

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:43.220
Session.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:45.860
When the new window opens up,
change the advertised route

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:49.520
priority to a value larger than
what was configured for HA VPN

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:50.150
tunnels.

00:10:50.150 --> 00:10:52.550
For example, my environment,
my HA VPN tunnels

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:54.100
have a value of 1,000.

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:56.720
So I'll configure the routing
priority for the classic VPN

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:59.170
tunnel with a value of 1,200.

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:01.340
Now before I click
Save and Continue,

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:03.650
I actually have a
on-premises router

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:06.240
where I need to make the
configuration changes as well.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:09.070
So that traffic is symmetrical
between my HA VPN tunnel

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:11.810
pair and my on-premises
router and Google.

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:13.850
So I'll configure that
in the background,

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:15.975
and then I'm going to click
Save and Continue here.

00:11:24.865 --> 00:11:27.490
So now that it's configured, if
we return to that hamburger bar

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:34.670
menu at the top left hand side,
scroll down to VPC network,

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:39.768
and select the Routes tab,
click the Dynamic tab,

00:11:39.768 --> 00:11:41.310
and now we can see
that I'm receiving

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:44.937
a route from the on-premises
network with priority 1,200.

00:11:44.937 --> 00:11:46.770
Now, this was the
priority that I configured

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:48.780
towards the on-prem
network as well,

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:52.530
so traffic is now preferring
my HA VPN tunnel pair.

00:11:55.670 --> 00:11:57.800
If I look below
here, and I return

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:00.950
to my ping running in the
background, if I stop it,

00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:03.710
I can see that traffic has
now effectively switched over

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:06.840
to my new HA VPN tunnel
without any interruptions.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:08.630
We can see here that
we have zero packet

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:12.470
loss between my ping sent
from the GCP environment

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:14.360
to my on-premises router.

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:19.160
Now if I restart the ping, I can
return to my VPN configuration

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:21.140
under the hybrid
connectivity section.

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:29.910
If I select the existing
classic VPN tunnel,

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:32.346
I can safely delete it now.

00:12:32.346 --> 00:12:34.320
Click Delete.

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:36.180
Are you sure you want
to delete this tunnel?

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:38.850
Click Delete.

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:41.890
Now since the traffic is safely
going across the HA VPN tunnel

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:45.350
pair, deleting the tunnel will
have no effect on my traffic.

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:50.400
So if I return to
the VPC Networks tab,

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:56.780
click the Routes button,
click the Dynamic tab,

00:12:56.780 --> 00:13:03.650
now I can see that I'm receiving
the prefix 192.168.125.0/24

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:06.720
only from my HA VPN tunnel pair.

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:08.790
So if we go back to the
pings you're being sent,

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:11.630
and I stop the pings, you
can see effectively I've

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:13.400
had zero packet
loss now that I've

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:16.250
removed my classic VPN tunnel.

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:18.350
And with that, we have
now a successful migration

00:13:18.350 --> 00:13:22.580
from classic VPN over to HA VPN.

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:25.790
That concludes the first
migration path from classic VPN

00:13:25.790 --> 00:13:29.850
utilizing BGP to HA VPN.

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:34.080
Section two, migrate an existing
classic VPN utilizing policy

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:38.220
based VPN to HA VPN between
two projects or VPCs

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:40.200
within Google Cloud.

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:43.260
Now in this section, we'll look
at configuring HA VPN tunnels

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:45.420
between two projects in GCP.

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:47.820
This migration path
is used to migrate

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:51.000
classic VPN tunnels
between two projects in GCP

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:53.010
to HA VPN tunnels.

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:57.300
Let's click the hamburger menu
at the top left hand side.

00:13:57.300 --> 00:14:01.850
Let's scroll down to Networking,
select Hybrid Connectivity,

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:05.700
click the VPN tab, and
here, as you can see,

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:09.570
I have established a classic
VPN policy based tunnel.

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:11.400
If I scroll to the
right, you can see here

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:13.335
that there is no dynamic
routes configured,

00:14:13.335 --> 00:14:15.060
and it's completely
policy based,

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:18.030
meaning I manually configured
my traffic selectors

00:14:18.030 --> 00:14:20.040
and traffic is going
between this VPC

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:24.290
and project to another
project and VPC within GCP.

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:26.370
Now, if I open
this new window, I

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:27.880
can show you I have
another project.

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:30.900
And in this project, I will go
to the same Hybrid Connectivity

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:34.320
section and the VPN tab
and show you the tunnels

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:35.310
that I have created.

00:14:35.310 --> 00:14:38.520
You can see here I have
a existing classic VPN

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:40.440
tunnel, which is policy based.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:45.760
If I scroll to the right, you
can see that it's established.

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:48.390
You can also see here that I
have existing tunnels created

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:50.640
because we're going to
do the configuration only

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:54.150
in one project, so I've already
configured my HA VPNs here

00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:55.990
in this project.

00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:59.220
We will now create HA VPN
tunnels to the new project

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:02.490
instead of to an
on-premises network.

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:04.470
Going back to the
original project,

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:06.660
I'll create a new
pair of HA VPN tunnels

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:09.950
by selecting Create VPN Tunnel.

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:12.290
I will then select the
HA VPN demo gateway

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:15.750
that I've created before
and click Continue.

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:18.050
So in this case, we'll
select a Peer VPN gateway

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:20.030
type of Google
Cloud, and here I'll

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:23.720
be presented with a list of
projects that I have access to,

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:25.220
and I'll select the
project that I'm

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:27.740
attempting to create a
connection to by selecting

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:29.060
the VPN gateway name.

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:39.460
Now, as you recall since I've
already created an HA VPN

00:15:39.460 --> 00:15:40.990
gateway in the
other project, this

00:15:40.990 --> 00:15:43.570
is something that you would have
had to do prior to the step.

00:15:43.570 --> 00:15:45.310
So you would simply
follow similar steps

00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:47.680
that I've shown you earlier
on how to create an HA VPN

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:49.780
gateway, and you would
do that in one project

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:50.812
before the other.

00:15:50.812 --> 00:15:52.270
Now once I select
the gateway name,

00:15:52.270 --> 00:15:54.640
here I'm presented with the
high availability options.

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:56.890
Again, very similar from
the previous section.

00:15:56.890 --> 00:15:59.170
Here, we will create
a pair of VPN tunnels

00:15:59.170 --> 00:16:00.880
again to provide
high availability

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:04.560
with a four nines of SLA.

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:07.020
Next, I will select my
existing Cloud routers.

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:11.070
Again, you can create a new
one, but it's not necessary.

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:13.250
And now once I click
the VPN tunnels,

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:16.160
you can see that they've
already been pre-associated

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:19.110
one to one with the interfaces
of my peer VPN gateway.

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:23.080
Now, this is done automatically
for you within Google Cloud.

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:25.670
Now again, similar to
before, let's give it a name.

00:16:25.670 --> 00:16:27.985
I'm going to call it
havpn-peer-project-0.

00:16:31.630 --> 00:16:33.880
Next, the same configuration
for IKE version.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:36.310
If you want, you
can change that.

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:38.950
Next, enter a pre-shared key.

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:41.470
Now again, a strong
pre-shared key is recommended.

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:44.360
See links for our documentation
below for more information,

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:47.020
but for this demo, I'm going to
select a simple pre-shared key.

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:52.670
Once entered, I click Done.

00:16:52.670 --> 00:16:54.800
Next, I'm going to
configure my second tunnel.

00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:56.758
By selecting this tunnel,
let's give it a name.

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:04.329
I'm going to name it
havpn-peer-project-1.

00:17:04.329 --> 00:17:09.940
Next, I'm going to enter the
same pre-shared key again.

00:17:13.470 --> 00:17:15.810
Click Done, then
Create and Continue.

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:22.710
On this page, we'll
create the BGP sessions.

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:24.720
I've already created
exactly the same thing here

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:27.450
in my other projects, so I'm
only going to do it here.

00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:29.580
Let's click Configure
and give it a name.

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:33.300
So in my example,
it's havpn-peer-0.

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:38.120
Peer ASN in my case is 65507.

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:39.750
And now for the
route priorities,

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:42.210
it'll be a little bit different
than the previous example,

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:44.760
because policy based routes
have a default priority

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:46.885
in Google Cloud of 1,000.

00:17:46.885 --> 00:17:48.510
So let's create a
route priority that's

00:17:48.510 --> 00:17:51.300
higher than the policy
based route, so a lower

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:53.500
numerical value, as you recall.

00:17:53.500 --> 00:17:56.870
So I will enter 100, which
is lower than the 1,000,

00:17:56.870 --> 00:17:59.670
and then I'll enter the relevant
Cloud Router and BGP router

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:00.810
peer IPs.

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:06.350
My Cloud Router
IP is 169.254.7.1,

00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:10.461
and my Peers is 169.254.7.2.

00:18:10.461 --> 00:18:12.840
Please note once again that
all changes should be done

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:15.040
during maintenance windows.

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:17.460
Click Save and Continue.

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:20.230
Let's configure the
second BGP session.

00:18:20.230 --> 00:18:21.300
Click Configure.

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:22.770
Again, enter a name.

00:18:22.770 --> 00:18:26.430
I'm going to name
it havpn-peer-1.

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:28.830
And again, enter your ASN.

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:32.870
In my case, it's 65507.

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:36.390
The multi exit discriminator
will be the same as before,

00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:37.910
so for me it's 100.

00:18:37.910 --> 00:18:40.740
Then configure the appropriate
Cloud Router and BGP peer IPs.

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:48.752
In my environment, it's
169.254.8.1 and 169.254.8.2.

00:18:48.752 --> 00:18:51.080
Again, click save and continue.

00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:53.612
Now, I won't click Save
BGP Configuration just yet,

00:18:53.612 --> 00:18:55.070
and that's because
I'd like to show

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:58.250
traffic is currently going
across the existing classic VPN

00:18:58.250 --> 00:19:00.100
tunnel.

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:03.630
And here, I'm going to
initiate a ping from my VM.

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:12.160
The IP address of my peers
projects VM is 10.30.30.2.

00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:14.050
So I'm going to leave
the ping running.

00:19:14.050 --> 00:19:16.752
I can now click Save
BGP configuration.

00:19:24.470 --> 00:19:26.780
Now here on this page, we
get the BGP summary page

00:19:26.780 --> 00:19:28.950
that you've seen previously.

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:31.390
You can click OK at the bottom.

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:33.610
Here you can see the
newly created tunnels,

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:36.160
and you can see the peer
VPN gateway and the project

00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:38.050
that it's associated with.

00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:43.060
If we scroll to the right, we
can see the current BGP session

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:45.050
status.

00:19:45.050 --> 00:19:50.570
If we click Refresh, we can see
the BGP peers are established.

00:19:50.570 --> 00:19:53.120
Click on the hamburger menu
at the top left hand side

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:57.590
and scroll down to VPC network
and select the Routes tab.

00:20:00.170 --> 00:20:05.180
Next, select the Dynamic
tab, and now we can see

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:10.280
a destination range
of 10.30.30.0/24,

00:20:10.280 --> 00:20:13.220
which is a new route that
was created across the HA VPN

00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:14.860
peers.

00:20:14.860 --> 00:20:17.980
If I click the All tab, I can
see a policy based drought

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:25.000
tunnel with priority 1,000 for
the same prefix, 10.30.30.0/24.

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:29.110
Now, traffic has automatically
moved over to the HA VPN pair

00:20:29.110 --> 00:20:32.070
across these two
priority 100 link.

00:20:32.070 --> 00:20:33.970
So if we return to
the Dynamic tab,

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:37.150
again, you can see these
two priority 100 link,

00:20:37.150 --> 00:20:41.630
and traffic is effectively moved
over to the HA VPN tunnels.

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:43.930
If I return to my VM
window down below,

00:20:43.930 --> 00:20:46.328
I can now stop
the pings, and you

00:20:46.328 --> 00:20:48.370
can see that traffic has
seamlessly switched over

00:20:48.370 --> 00:20:50.080
without any packet loss.

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:53.930
I can then
reestablish the pings,

00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:56.180
go back to my VPN
section, and simply

00:20:56.180 --> 00:21:00.330
select the VPN policy based
routing tunnel and delete it.

00:21:00.330 --> 00:21:02.750
So again, let's go back
to the hamburger bar menu,

00:21:02.750 --> 00:21:08.710
let's scroll down to Hybrid
Connectivity and VPN,

00:21:08.710 --> 00:21:11.290
select the classic
VPN policy based

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:13.300
routing tunnel, click Delete.

00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:15.160
Are you sure you want
to delete this tunnel?

00:21:15.160 --> 00:21:15.820
Click Delete.

00:21:19.403 --> 00:21:20.320
And there you have it.

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:23.770
We only have HA VPNs tunnel
left, so the last thing to do

00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:25.750
is to delete the
existing classic VPN

00:21:25.750 --> 00:21:28.670
gateways unless you're using
them for any other tunnels.

00:21:28.670 --> 00:21:31.090
But if you're not, you
can safely delete them.

00:21:31.090 --> 00:21:34.150
Now if we go back to my VM
ping running here below,

00:21:34.150 --> 00:21:36.190
you can see that if
I stop it, there's

00:21:36.190 --> 00:21:39.000
been no packet loss when
a tunnel was deleted.

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:40.660
So I can return to
my Cloud console,

00:21:40.660 --> 00:21:48.990
scroll down to the VPC
network sections and routes,

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:50.810
and you can see
here in the All tab,

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:54.920
I no longer see the policy based
route VPN tunnel for prefix

00:21:54.920 --> 00:22:00.110
10.30.30.0/24, and they're
only still being learned via

00:22:00.110 --> 00:22:05.440
the dynamic routes
in the Dynamic tab.

00:22:05.440 --> 00:22:07.450
And with that, this
concludes this video.

00:22:07.450 --> 00:22:09.730
I've posted some useful
links to our documentation

00:22:09.730 --> 00:22:12.610
below which explain various
topologies and configurations

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:13.857
with HA VPN.

00:22:13.857 --> 00:22:15.190
Thank you, and have a great day.

00:22:15.190 --> 00:22:19.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

